  "summary": "Four new works compare disease and normal states at pathway, tissue and cell-type resolution to prioritize genes or enzyme targets in cancer and type 2 diabetes.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is resolving how regulatory or pathway organization changes between conditions, then converting that into ranked candidates. The cancer preprint extends the N/T-gene framework to Reactome pathway deregulation scoring, with minimum spanning tree taxonomy and normal-tumor comparison. Two T2D preprints prioritize risk genes: CAS-X integrates fine-mapped eQTL PIP and allelic fold changes across five metabolic tissues, while CELL-X links fine-mapped GWAS genes to single-cell islet atlas data for beta-cell relevance. The published Cell Systems study uses multi-omics-constrained kinetic ensembles of BRCA1-deficient ovarian cancer cells to predict 28 enzyme vulnerabilities. The new element is explicit tissue, cell-type or pathway context in prioritization. The three preprints provide no independent validation in the summaries, so their conclusions remain provisional.",
